Interview Question

How does Spring Boot know what to configure?

Answer:

It checks the dependencies available in the project and automatically configures the required beans using auto-configuration.

Session 14: Application Setup
Main Class

Every Spring Boot project has one main class.

Example

@SpringBootApplication

public class DemoApplication {

public static void main(String[] args) {

SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class,args);

}

}
What is @SpringBootApplication?

It combines three annotations.

@Configuration

Marks configuration class.

@EnableAutoConfiguration

Enables Auto Configuration.

@ComponentScan

Scans all components.

Instead of writing three annotations,

Spring Boot provides one annotation.

@SpringBootApplication
What happens inside SpringApplication.run()?
SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class,args);

This starts the whole application.

Internally it performs these steps:

Step 1

Creates Spring Container.

Step 2

Scans components.

@Component

@Service

@Repository

@Controller
Step 3

Creates Beans.

Step 4

Performs Dependency Injection.

Step 5

Loads application.properties.

Step 6

Starts Embedded Tomcat (for web applications).

Step 7

Makes the application ready to accept requests.

Flow

main()

↓

SpringApplication.run()

↓

Create IOC Container

↓

Component Scan

↓

Create Beans

↓

Dependency Injection

↓

Load Properties

↓

Start Tomcat

↓

Application Ready
Role of Main Class

The Main Class is the entry point of the application. It starts the Spring Boot application and initializes the Spring container.

Interview Question

Why should the main class be in the root package?

Answer:

Because @ComponentScan scans the current package and all its sub-packages. Keeping the main class in the root package ensures all controllers, services, repositories, and other components are detected automatically.

Session 15: Runners in Spring Boot

Sometimes we want some code to execute immediately after the application starts.

Spring Boot provides two interfaces.

1. CommandLineRunner

Runs after the application starts.

Example

import org.springframework.boot.CommandLineRunner;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class MyRunner implements CommandLineRunner {

    @Override
    public void run(String... args) {
        System.out.println("Application Started!");
    }
}

Output:

Application Started!
2. ApplicationRunner

Works like CommandLineRunner but receives an ApplicationArguments object, which makes it easier to access named and unnamed command-line arguments.

Example

import org.springframework.boot.ApplicationArguments;
import org.springframework.boot.ApplicationRunner;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class MyAppRunner implements ApplicationRunner {

    @Override
    public void run(ApplicationArguments args) {
        System.out.println("Application Started!");
    }
}
Difference
CommandLineRunner	ApplicationRunner
Gets String... args	Gets ApplicationArguments
Simpler	Better argument handling
Mostly used	Used when command-line arguments need parsing
Real-world Uses
Insert default admin user.
Load initial data.
Print startup messages.
Check database connection.
Initialize cache.
Session 16: Logging & Banner Customization
What is Logging?

Logging means recording what happens in the application while it is running.

Instead of using:

System.out.println("Saved");

we use a logging framework.

Why Logging?
Debug issues
Monitor application
Record errors
Track user activity
What is Logback?

Spring Boot uses Logback as its default logging framework.

No extra setup is required.

Example
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class Demo {

    private static final Logger logger =
            LoggerFactory.getLogger(Demo.class);

    public void save() {

        logger.info("Employee Saved");

        logger.warn("Low Memory");

        logger.error("Database Error");
    }
}
Log Levels
Level	Purpose
TRACE	Very detailed information
DEBUG	Debugging during development
INFO	Normal application events
WARN	Warning messages
ERROR	Errors or failures
